writing:
’Tis but as if I said, “Not here--not there--
Not risen--not writing letters.” Well I know
What is this beauty men are babbling of;

wriggled:
Moonlighted pallor, ere she shrunk and blinked,
And, shuddering, wriggled down to the unclean;
Or, my own mother, leaving her last smile

wrists:
‘Marian! I find you. Shall I let you go?’
I held her two slight wrists with both my hands;
‘Ah Marian, Marian, can I let you go?’

wrinkle:
Ain't seen no sich fancy dressin' sence las' quah'tly meetin' day;
Gals all dressed in silks an' satins, not a wrinkle ner a crease,
Eyes a-battin', teeth a-shinin', haih breshed back ez slick ez grease;

writhing:
Too far for any touch, yet near enough
To view the writhing creature,—then at last;
‘Stand still there, in the holy Virgin’s name,

writ:
Though Thou forget me, though Thou hide Thy face,
And blot me from the Book which Thou hast writ;
Thy Name will I remember in my praise

writhe:
My voice, empaled upon rhyme’s golden hooks,
Not once would writhe, nor quiver, nor revolt;
I read on calmly,—calmly shut it up,

wriggles:
An' you min's yo' pap,
Ef you nevah wriggles
Outen Sukey's lap;

wringin:
Eight of 'em hyeah all tol' an' yet
Dese eyes o' mine is wringin' wet;
My haht's a-achin' ha'd an' so',
